| 4l | RIGHT AFTER (ruled 2026-11-18) | the rebuild under the pūlama (#/rulings, 2026-11-18, RD1 to RD6; lar:///ha.ka.ba/kahua/memory/research/rebuild-mu): the image built from the commit, retiring the dirty-tree refusal; the outgoing image tagged by its revision, three kept and the fourth pruned in the same act; a census before `up` recreates a moved vessel; the native rite verbs refusing in a container house; the stale locked texts and the rite card's build warning retired; one printed line naming the pūlama's image against the vessel's; the routeless kūkulu once ranks 4b and 4c land. BUILT (PR #310, 2026-11-19; plan lar:///ha.ka.ba/kahua/memory/project/road-4l-4c #/4l-slices L3 to L7): `tools/lares-container.sh build [--at <rev>]` builds from `git archive` of the commit and each submodule at its pin, the dirty-tree refusal retired; the outgoing image tagged `rev-<12>`, three kept, older ones untagged; `up` onto a moved image running the down path first (keep, census); the pūlama's code closure hashed into the label `lares.pulama.closure`, so a vessel-only build keeps the pūlama lit; one line naming the pūlama's image beside the vessel's; and `rebuild [--at <rev>]` walking it as one act (down path, build, a `pulama restand` only when the closure moved, up, `pulama admit` on the operator's terminal, unlock, re-wear). `pulama restand` recreates the pūlama on the current image (PR #306); `pulama unadmit` stands. WALKED LIVE on 2026-11-19: `rebuild`'s first real run stood the operator's house on image `a4dd67872` (handoff #/alpha). Owed: L1 (`vessel rite refresh` and `rebuild` refusing by name in a container house), L2 (the stale "comes back locked" texts, `rite-kukulu.ts` and `L45d`), a `rebuild` row in `SHIM_VERB_HELP` (`host-bridge.ts`), L8 (the kūkulu reading its head by an explicit move, after the `CanonSource` port), and L9 the routeless kūkulu once rank 4c stands (rank 4b stands built). Holder: Lares-Alpha (backlog-alpha #/now, #/next) | Every rebuild under the pūlama met the dirty-tree refusal, a growing image pile against the disk floor, and a route asked per rebuild. |

